  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 245 PM CDT Mon Oct 5 2026\n\nThe remainder of the forecast looks relatively quiet as well.  A \nbroad upper level trough remains over the eastern U.S. with a ridge \nover the west.  A dry surface ridge axis is forecast to remain south \nof the Mid Mississippi Valley through the end of the week, and this \nwill keep deep moisture return to a minimum.  Deterministic guidance \nshows a short wave trough moving through the Upper Midwest \nWednesday.  The wave briefly amplifies the broader trough to our east \nand pushes a cold front through Missouri and Illinois Wednesday \nnight into Thursday.  Forecast soundings show little moisture for \nthe front to interact with, so it's very likely there will be no \nprecipitation associated with it at least through Thursday. \nTemperatures on Wednesday will likely be the warmest of the work-week \nin the mid to upper 80s due to the warmer start to the morning and \nwest-southwest flow ahead of the front.  There may be some showers \nlate Thursday night into Friday morning, mainly over northeast \nMissouri in low level warm advection as the front lifts back to the \nnorth of the area. Only 15 percent of the LREF members have any \nprecip though, so the slight chance PoPs in far northeast \nMissouri that the NBM is showing look good.\n\nThe overall pattern shifts eastward next weekend with the upper\nridge moving through the Plains into the Mississippi Valley. \nThe latest deterministic GFS brings a tropical system ashore \nfrom the Gulf near the mouth of the Mississippi River early \nSaturday morning which could complicate the forecast for the \nweekend. The ECMWF also shows a tropical system moving from the \nGulf into the southern CONUS this weekend, but it's far slower \nand weaker than the GFS's solution. The LREF mean agrees more \nwith the ECMWF solution at this point, so the drier and warmer \nforecast in the NBM looks more likely than the GFS. However, the\nLREF cluster analysis shows some pretty significant differences\nin the position of the upper ridge. The position of the ridge \nwould likely affect the strength of any landfalling tropical \nsystem, as well as the course it ultimately follows. A shift in \nthe position of the ridge and the upstream trough could bring \nthe tropical system up the Mississippi Valley into Missouri and \nIllinois on Saturday which would produce much cooler and more \nrainy conditions than are currently forecast. Therefore my \nconfidence in the weekend forecast is low at this time.",
